# Lume GitHub analyzer — environment
#
# Copy to .env and adjust (or export these before `make run`).
# .env is gitignored — keep your real OWNER / GH_TOKEN out of the repo so the
# project can be shared publicly.

# Your GitHub login. Used as:
#   - the default owner until data/github/last_owner exists (a `make fetch`
#     run writes it)
#   - the ONLY owner whose follower/following lists get the water-account /
#     recruiter pre-screen (suspects.json) merged into /api/people
# Leave empty to use data/github/last_owner once present.
OWNER=

# Optional: raise the unauthenticated 60 req/h -> 5000 req/h limit on the
# GitHub API. Used by the server's live proxy (/api/live/github), the people
# refresh (/api/refresh), scripts/fetch-github.sh, people-scan.sh and
# unfollow.sh. NEVER commit a real token.
# GH_TOKEN=ghp_xxxxxxxxxxxx

# Optional: shift the server port if 8091 is taken.
# LUME_GITHUB_PORT=9000

# --- Agent (LLM chat) ---
# The /chat agent and the repo_insights / people tools use Lume's built-in LLM
# bridge (OpenAI-compatible endpoint). Leave all three unset to get the
# canned offline engine instead (works, but no real model answers).
#   LLM_API_URL  — endpoint, e.g. https://api.openai.com/v1 (or a local
#                  vLLM/Ollama-compatible server)
#   LLM_API_KEY  — secret; NEVER commit a real key
#   LLM_MODEL    — model id, e.g. gpt-4o-mini
#   LLM_TIMEOUT  — seconds to wait on the upstream LLM stream (default 60)
# LLM_API_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# LLM_API_KEY=sk-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# LLM_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
# LLM_TIMEOUT=60

# Optional: raw text APPENDED to the agent's system prompt (single line; the
# .env loader reads line by line, so no line breaks inside the value).
# Use it for scenario guidance the agent should always follow — e.g. which
# data to answer from, how to cite evidence, output language:
# LLM_SYSTEM_EXTRA="You are a GitHub talent-signal analyst. Base answers on
# the local snapshot (data/github/<owner>/snapshot.json) and /api/* results,
# cite concrete repo names and numbers as evidence, never invent data."
